  "query_preview": "..\n     Strong winds and dry conditions leading to potential Critical \nFire Weather...\n\nToday: A broad ridge is stretching across the Pacific Northwest with \nthe apex centered over Western MT. It will continue to  promote the \ndry, warm pattern that has been in the Inland Northwest since \nWednesday. 850mb max temperatures range from 22-24C on Friday. It \nwill keep temperatures in the upper 80 to 90s for most. Lewiston has \naround a 50% probability of reaching triple digits. Areas being \nimpacted by wildfire smoke could have a few degrees shaved off the \nforecast high. HeatRisk remains minor to moderate across the region. \nAfternoon winds will increase with weak afternoon thermal lows will \nbring brief increases to winds with gusts reaching into the teens. \nOvernight lows will be in the 60s.\n\nSaturday: It continues to be main focus for the upcoming weekend. \nThere is high confidence for the winds on Saturday. Ensembles \ncontinue to swing a strong +120kt upper level jet across WA as a Low \nswings into the Lower BC. Any chance of precip across the region \nwill limited to the Cascade Crest. The Low will push a strong dry \ncold front through the Inland Northwest with 850mb-500mb winds \nranging from 30-60kts. The winds will mix down to the surface as the \nfront passes. Westerly winds will begin to increase in the late \nmorning and continue into the evening. Timing for the strongest \nwinds will be in the afternoon to early evening hours. Ensembles \nhave been very consistent with speeds and timing. For speeds, \nsustained winds will be in the 20 to low 30 mph range. Weaker winds \nin the teens will be over the Lower Idaho Panhandle. For gusts, a \nrange of 30 to low 40s for most. The Lower Idaho Panhandle will have \ngusts into the 20s. Outside of the Lower Idaho Panhandle, \nprobability of wind gusts greater than 35 mph is at least 60 \npercent. Southeast Washington, Lewiston, and Western Columbia Basin \nincluding Waterville Plateau down to Wenatchee has 20-40 percent \nprobability for greater than 50 mph gusts. Outside of fire weather \nconcerns, impacts include blowing dust and minor tree and property \ndamage. It will be something to keep an eye on for the next model \nruns. Highs for the day will be in the slightly cooler with a range \nof upper 70s to low 90s. Overnight lows will be in the 40s to 50s.\n\nSunday: While temperatures will be a lot cooler, winds will continue \nto be breezy. The region will see winds sustained in the teens with \ngusts into the 20s continuing to impact ongoing and new fires in the \narea. Winds will shift to out of the north and funnel down the \nOkanogan across the Waterville Plateau bringing locally higher \nspeeds with gusts into the 30s. Highs will be in the 70s to mid 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 40s to mid 50s.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: A flat ridge will fill in behinds the \nexiting Low. Temperatures will rebound back into the 80s to low 90s. \nWinds will continue the diurnal trend of breezy afternoons and calm \novernights. The warm, dry pattern will continue with no chances of \nbeneficial precip for the start of August. Overnight lows will be in \nthe 50s. /JDC",
